      "note": "Refreshed 2026-07-31 deep run -- prior cache (as_of 07-28) window predated the violent 07-28 crash and 07-29/30 rally entirely. Basis of stop_loss_framework (stop = 2x ATR20, floored at 3%). | 2026-08-12: added the 6 names that had no entry, incl. 2 brand-new positions (STM, TXN) and 2 that had been silently uncapped for weeks (FLTW, BX). ATR computed over 20 of 23 available bars via the policy's own (high-low)/close rule. | IONQ 6.04% added 2026-08-17 by smith-book on first appearance (21 daily obs, batched with SMH); shorter window than the rest of this table, treat as directional.",
      "ttl_days": 7,
      "refresh_after": "2026-08-19",
      "method": "simple mean of the last 20 true ranges / last close"
    },
    "earnings_method": "RESOLVED 2026-07-28 (G20): reliable method is yfinance get_earnings -> take the latest quarterly reportedDate and add ~91 days for the next print; cross-check near-dated names against the FMP earnings-calendar endpoint (works on the current plan for the calendar range query; the per-symbol earnings-company endpoint is plan-blocked). yfinance's own earnings-calendar endpoint remains unreliable -- do not use it. | 2026-08-07 audit: web-verified 5 heuristic dates -- 4 correct (MP/AMAT/NBIS/MKSI), 1 wrong by 16 days (BABA 08-12 est vs 08-28 actual). The +91d cadence heuristic is right ~80% of the time but its misses are large enough to produce false proximity flags; verify any date that is about to gate a decision.",
    "beta_method": "RESOLVED 2026-07-28 (G15): betas computed vs SMH (the book's true factor, not SPX) from 20 daily returns, 25 Jun - 24 Jul 2026. CAVEAT: a 20-observation window carries wide standard errors -- treat these as ordinal (which names are high- vs low-beta) rather than precise point estimates, and refresh to a 60-day window on the next deep run. No name now defaults to 1.0. TTL 30 days; refresh after 2026-08-27.",
    "sentiment_method": "RESOLVED-IN-PART 2026-07-28 (G17). Findings: (a) 52-week high/low from WEEKLY bars is EXACT, not an approximation -- a weekly high is by construction the max of that week's daily highs, so the 52wk range component never had an error; (b) RSI14 must use TRUE DAILY closes -- the weekly-aggregated version read 53.12 against a true daily 35.81, a 17.3-point error that materially misstated the composite; (c) the 125-day MA still uses weekly closes, a genuine but small smoothing approximation. METHOD: indices auto-aggregate to weekly for period>=3mo; use period='1mo' with at most 3 symbols per call to force daily bars. RSI14 needs only 15 daily closes, so one 1mo call suffices.",
    "cache_policy": {
      "added": "2026-07-28",
      "rationale": "A quick run was costing ~119KB of raw API payload (~33k tokens), of which 18 of 27 calls were avoidable. ATR20, betas and earnings dates are slow-moving; re-pulling them every run is the single largest waste.",
      "ttl_days": {
        "atr20": 7,
        "betas": 30,
        "earnings_calendar": "until the stored date passes",
        "ticker_map": 365,
        "fomc_cache": "until next_check_date",
        "etf_constituents": 30,
        "analyst_targets": 7,
        "rsi14": 7,
        "rel_strength_1m": 7
      },
      "refresh_rule": "On a QUICK run, read every cache as-is; refresh nothing -- EXCEPT that rsi14 and rel_strength_1m are now TTL-enforced regardless of run mode (fixed 2026-08-12): both feed live proposal triggers, and a deep-run-only refresh against a 7-day TTL meant they were routinely stale precisely when a trigger needed them (rel_strength was found 12 days old with a benchmark return 18.6pp wrong). If either is past refresh_after, refresh it or suppress its triggers -- cmd_triggers enforces this itself via TRIGGER_CACHE_MAX_AGE_DAYS and emits empty candidate lists rather than acting on stale technicals. On a DEEP run, refresh every entry whose TTL has expired. ATR20 over a 20-day window drifts roughly 5% per week -- weekly refresh is ample, and a stale ATR makes stops slightly wide, which is the safe direction to be wrong.",
      "price_source_rule": "Live prices: use yfinance get_stock_price (232 chars/symbol). Use INDmoney get_us_stocks_details (726 chars/symbol, 3.1x heavier) ONLY when market_session is pre-open and ext_hr_live_price is genuinely needed. get_stock_price already carries price, prev_close and day high/low -- everything the stop framework consumes.",
      "session_reuse": "Do not re-pull prices for the same symbol within 20 minutes unless the user asks for a fresh check or a stop level is being placed."
    },
